   "text": "The drawing shows proline: a five-membered ring made of one NH and four carbons, with a carboxylic acid (C=O and OH) on the ring carbon next to nitrogen.\n\n**Ring and substituents**\n- The ring has N\u2013H at the top, two plain CH2 carbons on the left and bottom, and the substituted carbon at the right.\n- The substituted ring carbon carries the COOH group, drawn with a single wedge. This is the only stereocentre.\n\n**Stereochemistry**\n- The wedge is narrow at the ring carbon and widens toward the carboxyl carbon, so the COOH points toward the viewer and the implicit H points away.\n- CIP priorities at the stereocentre are N > COOH > ring CH2 > H.\n- On the page, N is at upper left, COOH at right and CH2 at lower left. N \u2192 COOH \u2192 CH2 therefore runs clockwise with H in the back, which gives R. This is D-proline.\n\n**SMILES check**\n- For `OC(=O)[C@H]1CCCN1`, the neighbour order at the stereocentre is COOH, H, N (via ring closure 1), CH2.\n- `@` means H \u2192 N \u2192 CH2 runs counterclockwise when viewed from COOH. Swapping to put H first turns this into `@@`, so COOH \u2192 N \u2192 CH2 is clockwise when viewed from H.\n- Viewed with H pointing away, that rotation reverses, so N \u2192 COOH \u2192 CH2 is clockwise, which is R. This matches the drawing.\n\nSMILES: OC(=O)[C@H]1CCCN1"
